{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Albany-area commuter (as opposed to streetcar) rail service, distinct from the UTC city lines in albany.geojson. Route geometry follows the real corridor documented for the Rensselaer & Saratoga Railroad / D&H line (Troy-Green Island-Waterford-Mechanicville-Round Lake-Malta-Ballston Spa, then north to Saratoga Springs), extended south to Albany Union Station. This corridor's passenger-service history is much longer than a single company name suggests, so it's split into two features sharing (approximately) the same geometry: the original independent Rensselaer & Saratoga Railroad (1836-1871), and the Delaware & Hudson era after D&H leased and later formally merged the line (1871-1962). Frequency (headway_min) is NOT sourced for either era \u2014 it's a rough era-appropriate estimate, not a documented timetable. The Albany-Binghamton features that used to live in this file (the independent Albany and Susquehanna era and the D&H Susquehanna Division) have been MOVED OUT to albany_and_susquehanna_railroad.geojson, where they are drawn on the real digitized right-of-way instead of the 20-point waypoint sketch used here; that is a 142-mile intercity main line rather than Albany commuter service, so it no longer belongs in this file.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rensselaer_and_Saratoga_Railroad (chartered April 14 1832; opened Troy-Ballston Spa March 19 1836; leased by D&H May 1 1871; formally merged into D&H January 30 1945)", "https://www.bridge-line.org/timeline (D&H corporate timeline confirming the 1871 lease and 1832 incorporation dates)", "https://www.newyorkalmanack.com/2020/08/early-railroads-from-the-capital-district-to-saratoga/ (route via Rensselaer & Saratoga RR)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Railway (Albany-Saratoga commuter train discontinued late 1962)", "https://penneyvanderbilt.wordpress.com/2016/02/26/delaware-hudson-passenger-train-demise/ (by 1960 D&H ran a commuter train Albany-Saratoga Springs; two named trains, 5 and 40, dropped around 1960 ahead of full discontinuation in 1962)", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Rensselaer_and_Saratoga_Railroad.map and https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Saratoga_and_Schenectady_Rail_Road.map (CC BY 4.0) -- digitized right-of-way replacing the waypoint sketches previously used for both features in this file"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for either era (departure times, number of daily round trips), and the specific year general passenger service on this corridor started being run/marketed as a commuter service rather than ordinary regional passenger service.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Rensselaer and Saratoga Railroad", "line": "Rensselaer and Saratoga: Troy \u2013 Ballston Spa (independent era)", "id": "rs-troy-ballston-spa", "color": "#5d6d7e", "mode": "commuter_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 32, "speed_source": "estimated: early steam-era regional rail, frequent stops, rougher track; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1836, "end": 1871, "headway_min": 480, "note": "estimated ~1-2 trains/day, typical of 1830s-1860s regional passenger rail; unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rensselaer_and_Saratoga_Railroad", "https://www.bridge-line.org/timeline", "https://www.newyorkalmanack.com/2020/08/early-railroads-from-the-capital-district-to-saratoga/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware_and_Hudson_Railway", "https://penneyvanderbilt.wordpress.com/2016/02/26/delaware-hudson-passenger-train-demise/", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Rensselaer_and_Saratoga_Railroad.map", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Data:Saratoga_and_Schenectady_Rail_Road.map"]}], "geometry_source": "Digitized right-of-way from Wikimedia Commons (Data:Rensselaer and Saratoga Railroad.map, CC BY 4.0): the Troy-Waterford and Waterford-Ballston Spa traces joined at their shared endpoint, 265 points, 24.6 mi. Replaces a 6-point waypoint sketch.", "geometry_quality": "Real-traced throughout, 265 points, median vertex spacing under 100 m. Traced length 24.6 mi; no documented mileage found for the Troy-Ballston Spa line to check it against."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-73.68598, 42.73309], [-73.68629, 42.73367], [-73.6871, 42.7344], [-73.68731, 42.73452], [-73.68915, 42.73546], [-73.68994, 42.73582], [-73.69033, 42.73598], [-73.69184, 42.73651], [-73.69331, 42.73709], [-73.69447, 42.73772], [-73.69508, 42.73832], [-73.69519, 42.73855], [-73.69526, 42.73882], [-73.69527, 42.73903], [-73.69522, 42.73929], [-73.69484, 42.74025], [-73.69361, 42.74364], [-73.69272, 42.74603], [-73.69233, 42.74724], [-73.69179, 42.74876], [-73.68998, 42.7537], [-73.68896, 42.75643], [-73.68818, 42.75846], [-73.68791, 42.75936], [-73.6877, 42.76021], [-73.68737, 42.76261], [-73.68708, 42.76457], [-73.68676, 42.76618], [-73.68657, 42.76679], [-73.68649, 42.7671], [-73.68556, 42.7695], [-73.68462, 42.77173], [-73.68443, 42.77228], [-73.68385, 42.77368], [-73.68379, 42.77392], [-73.68191, 42.77804], [-73.68113, 42.7794], [-73.68046, 42.78078], [-73.6803, 42.78192], [-73.68023, 42.78339], [-73.68022, 42.78392], [-73.68017, 42.78469], [-73.68011, 42.78494], [-73.67974, 42.78564], 